\chapter{The domain: money that must always balance}

A \emph{ledger} records movements of value between accounts. Its one
inviolable law is \emph{double entry}: every transaction posts equal and
opposite amounts, so the sum of all balances never changes. A system that
can violate this law is not a ledger with a bug --- it is not a ledger.

\section{The problem}

We want the smallest honest core of a ledger: open accounts, post
transactions between them, read balances --- with the double-entry invariant
enforced \emph{at write time}, so an unbalanced transaction cannot be
committed at all. Nightly reconciliation against a spreadsheet is exactly the
failure mode we refuse: by the time a mismatch is found, wrong numbers have
already been read and acted upon. The invariant belongs in the code that
writes, not in a report that reads.

\section{Vocabulary}

Every term below becomes part of the system's language --- the \emph{domain}
prefix of a chunk name, and an entry in the glossary. If a later chunk name
uses a word, that word is defined here.

\begin{description}
  \item[Account] a named store of value. Its \textbf{balance} is an integer
    number of \textbf{cents}; we never use floating point, because in a
    ledger rounding is not cosmetic but a correctness failure.
  \item[Posting] a single movement of a signed amount (in cents) into one
    account. A positive amount is a debit to that account's balance, a
    negative one a credit.
  \item[Transaction] an ordered set of postings that \textbf{must sum to
    zero}. This is double entry stated arithmetically.
  \item[Invariant] the property the system guarantees at all times: the sum
    of every account balance equals zero, because it started at zero and only
    balanced transactions were ever applied.
\end{description}
